    <h3 class="card-title">PDN and Slammer Circuit — Full Lab Report</h3>

    <p class="card-desc">
      This report studies how sudden current switching affects a 9 V power rail using a slammer circuit.
      The experiment compares slow and fast switching edges, estimates load current and Thevenin resistance,
      and evaluates how 1 µF and 1000 µF decoupling capacitors reduce voltage droop when placed near or far
      from the switching transistor.
    </p>

    <div class="findings">
      <div class="findings-title">Main Results</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Fast switching caused a much larger voltage drop than slow switching.</li>
        <li>Without decoupling, the fast-edge voltage drop reached about 1.811 V.</li>
        <li>A 1 µF capacitor reduced the drop but was not enough for strong stabilization.</li>
        <li>A 1000 µF capacitor placed near the transistor gave the best result.</li>
        <li>Capacitor placement close to the switching device reduced loop inductance and improved performance.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

